# The Baron's Island (side quest, docs/story/side_quests.md): out in the reeds of Lake Zarovich is an island nobody
# fishes near. Some nights the Baron's boat rows out to it with somebody in it who was unhappy too loudly about the
# festival, and rows back with nobody. **Twist:** they didn't drown. A dozen of Vallaki's unhappy are alive on the
# island, living on reeds and fish, and they are the only happy people within a day's walk of the town.
# **Escalation:** tonight the boat comes back with another, and the boatmen see the cookfires, and have orders for
# that too. The Baron, Izek, the festival and Ireena are only read, never set. Reached from Old Nistor at the landing
# from level 5, while the town isn't the council's (lake_zarovich/old_fisher:start), and the island
# (data/locations/zarovich_reed_island.json). Every line here is unvoiced for now.

# --- Old Nistor at the landing (lake_zarovich/old_fisher:start jumps here) ---

~ nistor
Old Nistor: The Reed Isle. (He doesn't look at it.) Nobody fishes near it. Some nights the Baron's boat rows out there from the town end of the lake, with somebody in the bottom of it tied up like washing.
Old Nistor: Somebody who said the festival was a waste of good wicker, maybe. Or laughed at the wrong moment. The boat comes back with nobody in it. We don't ask. Asking's unhappy.
Old Nistor: Take my boat if you want to look. It leaks on the left. Everything I own leaks on the left.
quest the_barons_island asked
-> END

# --- Ostap on the island (npc reed_isle_ostap) ---

~ ostap
if quest.the_barons_island == done
-> ostap_done
endif
if flag.reed_isle_boatmen_beaten
-> ostap_after
endif
if flag.reed_isle_met
Ostap: Keep your voice down when the moon's up. Sound carries on the water. That's how they'll find us, one night.
# Leaving before the boat came isn't the end of it (Wiki finding 43).
* Come back with us. We'll get you home. -> home_ask
* (Leave them to their fires.) -> END
endif
set reed_isle_met
Narrator: In the middle of the island, among the reeds, there are huts: little bent-over things of reed and driftwood, a dozen of them, and cookfires banked low, and fish drying on lines. A big bald man with a beard like a bird's nest stands up from the nearest fire with a fish-spear in his hand.
Ostap: You're not the Baron's. The Baron's don't land. (He looks you over.) Ostap. Cooper, once. I said at the Blue Water that the festival was a waste of good wicker, and the Baron's men took me out of my bed that night.
Ostap: They rowed me out here and tipped me in the shallows with my hands tied, and rowed home. There were six of us here already, then. Now there are fourteen. We fish. We sing. Nobody has to be happy on a Thursday.
if guest:ireena
Ostap: (He sees Ireena, and goes quiet, and then bows, badly, the way a man does who hasn't bowed in years.) Lady. They said in town you were safe at the church. I'm glad of it.
endif
* [Insight DC 12] (Look at the faces round the fires.) -> faces | faces_missed
* Come back with us. We'll get you home. -> home_ask
* (Leave them to their fires.) -> END

~ faces
Narrator: Thin faces, sunburnt and windburnt, and every one of them easy in a way nobody in Vallaki is. A woman is mending a net and humming. Two men are arguing about fish, cheerfully, and losing their tempers, and nobody is watching to see.
-> home_ask

~ faces_missed
Narrator: Fourteen castaways round three small fires. That's all you can tell.
-> home_ask

~ home_ask
Ostap: Home? (He laughs.) To be happy on Thursdays? No. But if you mean to help, there's a thing. The Baron's boat comes tonight. Another one for us. We pull them out of the shallows before they drown, every time.
Ostap: Only the last time, a boatman saw the smoke. If they see it again they'll tell the Baron, and the Baron doesn't leave a thing half done.
* (Wait at the landing for the boat.) -> boat
* Take them off before it comes, in Nistor's boat. -> vote_call
* (Leave them to it.) -> END

~ boat
Narrator: After dark a boat comes out of the mist from the town end of the lake, rowing hard, with something tied up in the bottom of it. It doesn't stop in the shallows. It runs up onto the shingle, and the men in it jump out with clubs and a lantern, looking for the smoke.
if flag.vallaki_backed == "wachter"
Narrator: They're wearing Lady Wachter's grey now, not the Baron's colours. The island changed hands with the town, that's all.
endif
interject tag:blunt: Fourteen people, and they've brought clubs.
combat reed_isle_boatmen

# --- After the boatmen (stage defended) ---

~ ostap_after
Narrator: The castaways have pulled the newest of them out of the boat and cut her hands free: a young woman who laughed at the Baron's sun, she says, and isn't laughing now. The boatmen's boat is pulled up into the reeds.
Ostap: Now there's a boat on our island. (He looks at it as if it might bite.) We could go home. We could go anywhere. We could stay.
* Stay. Keep the boat hidden, and nobody in town will know where it went. -> stay
* Come home with us. We'll walk you in through the gate ourselves. -> home

~ stay
set reed_isle_stayed
quest the_barons_island done
Ostap: Stay. Yes. (A woman at the fire cheers, and then puts her hand over her mouth, and then laughs because she doesn't have to.) We'll row out at night for salt and come back before light. The Baron can count his drowned.
-> gift

~ home
set reed_isle_home
quest the_barons_island done
Ostap: Home. (He says it like a word in a language he used to speak.) Then we'll row in by daylight, all fourteen, singing, and see who stops us.
-> gift

~ gift
Ostap: The boatmen brought these for her. (A pair of heavy black shackles, cold and too smooth, with no keyhole you can see.) The Baron keeps a box of them. I'd rather they were on the right wrists, one day.
-> gift_give

# The shackles are given in one place for all four endings (a lasting item is placed once: tests/unit/test_loot_distinct.gd).
~ gift_give
give dimensional_shackles
gold +80
approve thistle +3 wren_featherfoot +2 godrick_pendlebrook +2 kip_smudgewick +1: You stood on the Reed Isle with the Baron's castaways
-> END

~ ostap_done
Ostap: Fourteen, and the girl makes fifteen. We row out for salt at night. Nobody has to be happy. Come and eat fish sometime.
-> END

# --- Without a fight: the vote, then the crossings (or hiding the island) (NPC plan batch 4) ---
# The castaways vote on whether to go home at all (Persuasion either way, or let them decide). To go, the party rows
# them across in three crossings with a roll each (oars, staying dark, bailing); two crossings seen and the boatmen are
# on them. To stay, the party hides the island (Stealth or Survival); a missed roll and the boat lands as before.

~ vote_call
Narrator: Ostap strikes an iron kettle with a driftwood stick until the fourteen gather round the low, sputtering fires.
Narrator: They wrap themselves in damp wool and watch you with the quiet, unhurried eyes of people who have already been drowned once.
Ostap: Our guests have a boat, and the Baron's men have noticed our smoke. The boat leaks on the left, which is fair enough.
Ostap: They offer to row us back to shore before the watch comes out tonight, or help us pull the reeds over our heads and stay.
-> vote_speech

~ vote_speech
Ostap: Nobody leaves this mud because they are frightened on a dark night. We left Vallaki tied at the wrists, and we will leave here on our own feet or not at all.
Ostap: In town there are roofs that keep the rain off, and dry bread, and neighbours who look at their boots when the guards pass.
Ostap: Out here the perch is bony and the wind comes straight through your coat, but if a man wants to look miserable on a Thursday, nobody hits him with a stick.
Ostap [weary]: Speak if you have a mind to, and then we raise hands. Even the Baron never gave us a vote before a soaking.
* [Persuasion DC 12] (Argue that they should go home.) -> argue_go_ok | argue_go_miss
* [Persuasion DC 12] (Argue that they should stay hidden.) -> argue_stay_ok | argue_stay_miss
* (Let them decide for themselves.) -> vote_go

~ argue_go_ok
Narrator: You speak of winter coming down from the mountains, when the reeds will freeze hard and the lake will skin over with black ice.
Narrator: An old woman rubbing her knuckles by the embers nods once and says, "I left three good pots under my stairs, and my sister's boy has none."
Ostap: The winter is a hard argument to shout down. Well then, let us see the hands.
-> vote_go

~ argue_go_miss
Narrator: You speak of warm beds and dry floors in town, but a narrow-shouldered cobbler spits into the ash and says, "A dry floor is still the Baron's floor."
Narrator: Around the fire, heads shake slowly in the mist. Their coats are wet, not their spirits.
-> vote_stay

~ argue_stay_ok
Narrator: You tell them that four strokes of mud and an armful of cut sedge will hide this island better than any wall in Vallaki ever hid them.
Narrator: A girl with reed-plaited hair looks toward the dark water and says, "Let them row out for nothing. My feet know this mud now."
Ostap [smile]: Spoken like a proper frog. Let us have the hands, then.
-> vote_stay

~ argue_stay_miss
Narrator: You tell them the reeds will keep them safe if the fires die, but an old dyer coughs into his sleeve and says, "Safe and cold are cousins, friend."
Narrator: Several eyes turn toward the dark line of the shore where the town roofs lie out of the wind.
-> vote_go

~ vote_go
Ostap: Nine for the shore, five for the reeds. We pack what fits in our pockets and leave the kettles for the crows.
Narrator: Nobody cheers. They tie their bundles with eel-skin twine and kick wet sand over the coals in silence.
-> ferry_start

~ vote_stay
Ostap: Ten for the mud, four for the road. We stay where we are, and tonight we are only frogs and sedge.
Narrator: The fourteen rise without hurry, already reaching for wet sacks and buckets to smother the embers.
-> hide_start

~ ferry_start
if day
time until 19
endif
set ferry_trips = 0
set ferry_seen = 0
Narrator: Four of the castaways step into Old Nistor's skiff at dusk, settling between the ribs until the gunwales sit three fingers above the black water.
Narrator: "Mind the third plank," whispers a baker with grey knees, "she weeps around the nails."
Narrator: You push off from the shingle, and the reed island vanishes behind you after three strokes.
-> cross

~ cross
set ferry_trips += 1
if flag.ferry_trips == 1
Narrator: The mist lies flat on Lake Zarovich, and the Vallaki shore ahead is only a darker smear against the hills.
elif flag.ferry_trips == 2
Narrator: The second load is heavier by one cooper's bench and an iron pot nobody would leave behind, and the night has grown colder.
else
Narrator: Ostap steps in last, nearly putting the bow under, and sets his broad back against the stern with a wooden bailer in his fist.
endif
* [Athletics DC 12] (Take the oars.) -> oars_ok | oars_bad
* [Stealth DC 12] (Keep everyone low and the boat dark.) -> low_ok | low_bad
* [Acrobatics DC 12] (Bail the left side while you row.) -> bail_ok | bail_bad

~ oars_ok
Narrator: You catch the dark water cleanly and pull long, steady strokes that move the heavy skiff without a sound.
-> crossed

~ oars_bad
Narrator: A blade catches a submerged root and splashes hard, and the left oarlock gives a long, dry screech across the water.
set ferry_seen += 1
-> crossed

~ low_ok
Narrator: Everyone bends low between the thwarts until the boat looks like nothing more than a drifted log in the fog.
-> crossed

~ low_bad
Narrator: A distant lantern sweeps the mist, and its yellow light catches the wet blade of your raised oar.
set ferry_seen += 1
-> crossed

~ bail_ok
Narrator: Tin cup and wooden scoop work in time with your knees, keeping the black bilge down below the ankle-bones.
-> crossed

~ bail_bad
Narrator: Cold lake water rises over the floorboards on the left side, sloshing heavy and slow against your boots with every pull.
set ferry_seen += 1
-> crossed

~ crossed
if flag.ferry_trips < 3
-> cross
endif
if flag.ferry_seen >= 2
-> seen
endif
-> ferried

~ seen
Narrator: Across the water, a horn lantern swings up into a bow and holds steady, pointing straight at your wake.
Narrator: "Rowers off the reeds!" a voice barks over the flat water, followed by the heavy thud of four oars dropping into iron pins.
Narrator: Their prow cuts the fog toward the shingle, fast and straight, and there is no hiding the boat now.
combat reed_isle_boatmen

~ ferried
xp fight reed_isle_boatmen
set reed_isle_home
quest the_barons_island done
Narrator: All fourteen stand dripping among the gravel and willow roots by the Vallaki road as the grey morning seeps through the fog.
Narrator: Someone starts an old cellar-song about a barrel that would not hold water, and three more voices take it up before the first verse ends.
Narrator: They walk toward the town gates in their wet boots, singing in plain daylight where the watchmen cannot pretend not to see them.
Ostap [smile]: Fourteen in a bunch is harder to drop in a lake than one at a time. The Baron will have to buy a bigger boat.
Ostap: Come see me if you ever need a cask hooped properly. If I am back in the stocks, knock twice on the timber.
-> gift_ferried

~ gift_ferried
Ostap: I kept these from the night they tipped us in the lake. Cold iron, too smooth by half, and not a keyhole on them.
Ostap [weary]: Now we're safe ashore, I have no use for them. Put them on the right wrists if you get the chance.
-> gift_give

~ hide_start
Narrator: You haul lake water in wooden buckets to drown the cooking pits while the castaways pull down their drying lines of gutted perch.
Narrator: Working knee to knee in the dark, you lash cut bundles of sedge over the low willow huts until the roofs match the bank.
Narrator: Fourteen people lie flat in the cold mud beneath the reeds, breathing into their sleeves.
* [Stealth DC 12] (Smother every fire and hide every hut.) -> hide_ok | hide_bad
* [Survival DC 12] (Bank the fires so no smoke shows.) -> hide_ok | hide_bad

~ hide_ok
Narrator: The last steam dies into the mist without a smell of woodsmoke, and from ten paces off the island is only black water and broken rushes.
Narrator: Even the frogs begin to croak again along the shallows.
-> boat_passes

~ hide_bad
Narrator: A knot of pitch-pine deep in the ash refuses to die, sending a thin, stubborn ribbon of grey smoke straight up into the still air.
Narrator: In the marsh silence, the hiss of wet wood sounds loud as a forge.
-> boat

~ boat_passes
xp fight reed_isle_boatmen
set reed_isle_stayed
quest the_barons_island done
Narrator: The Baron's heavy boat grates on the gravel bar, and two guards hold up a lantern that shows nothing but empty sedge and wet stones.
Narrator: "Told you it was marsh-gas," one mutters, and they heave a bound woman over the side into the knee-deep water before pushing off again.
Narrator: As soon as their oar-strokes fade into the fog, four pairs of hands reach out of the reeds to haul her up and cut the cord at her wrists.
Narrator: Someone wraps a dryish sack around her shoulders and says softly, "You are all right now, neighbour. Make it fifteen."
Ostap: Gone back to their warm guardhouse, and left us another neighbour for our trouble.
Ostap [smile]: Fifteen is an awkward number for four huts, mind you. Somebody will have to sleep with their feet out the door.
Ostap: You kept our heads dry tonight, friends. Whenever Vallaki grows too cheerful for your stomach, there is perch on the coals.
-> gift_stayed

~ gift_stayed
Ostap: These came off the woman they brought ashore. Cold iron, too smooth by half, and not a keyhole on them.
Ostap [weary]: Take them with you. I'd sleep better knowing they found their way back to the wrists that earned them.
-> gift_give
